## 🧪 2. Data Preparation & Tokenization

### How Tokenization Works in your Code
Your code calls `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("distilbert-base-uncased")`.
*   **Vocabulary Type:** It uses **WordPiece Tokenization**. It breaks words into sub-word units (e.g., "embeddings" -> `["em", "##bed", "##dings"]`). This solves the **Out-Of-Vocabulary (OOV)** problem because unknown words can be reconstructed from common sub-words.
*   **Padding & Truncation:** 
    *   You set `max_length=128`. Any user query longer than 128 tokens is truncated (cut off).
    *   Shorter queries are padded with `[PAD]` tokens. The **Attention Mask** (a binary 1/0 array) tells the model's self-attention mechanism to ignore these `[PAD]` tokens so they don't skew predictions.

### Dataset Splitting Strategy
```python
train_test_split(..., test_size=0.2, random_state=42, stratify=labels)
```
*   **Stratify:** This is critical. It guarantees that the 80/20 train/test split maintains the *exact same ratio* of classes (`general`, `github`, `integration`) in both sets. This prevents data bias where the validation set accidentally has zero samples of a rare class.

