## 💬 5. Top 5 Killer Interview Questions & Answers

### Q1: "Why did you choose an F1-score metric over raw Accuracy?"
> **Answer:** "Accuracy is deceptive when you have imbalanced datasets. If 80% of my data belongs to the `general` class, a dumb model that predicts `general` every single time gets 80% accuracy but is useless. F1-score is the harmonic mean of Precision (how many predicted hits were true hits) and Recall (how many true hits did we capture). Using a *weighted F1-score* ensures all classes contribute fairly to the metric based on their support count."

### Q2: "What is the classification head of a sequence classifier like DistilBERT?"
> **Answer:** "The base model outputs a 768-dimensional vector representation for the first token, which is the `[CLS]` (Classification) token. This vector represents the semantic summary of the entire sentence. I appended a classification head on top of this: a Linear layer that projects the 768 dimensions down to 3 outputs (one per class), followed by a Softmax function to turn those raw outputs (logits) into probabilities."

### Q3: "Did you use Dynamic or Static Quantization?"
> **Answer:** "I used **Dynamic Quantization**. In dynamic quantization, the model weights are quantized to integer (INT8) at-rest, but the activations are dynamically quantized to INT8 during the forward pass. This is standard and ideal for NLP/Transformer workloads because dynamic quantization doesn't require a calibration dataset, unlike static quantization."

### Q4: "Why uncased model (`distilbert-base-uncased`) instead of cased?"
> **Answer:** "For intent classification, capitalization doesn't change the meaning. 'how to sync slack' has the same intent as 'How to sync Slack'. Lowercasing everything makes the model invariant to capitalization and reduces the vocabulary footprint, making training more stable."

### Q5: "If your model gets a query like 'Where is the db config file?', how does it know it belongs to 'github' and not 'general'?"
> **Answer:** "Because BERT represents words contextually. The self-attention mechanism correlates the phrase 'db config file' with other codebase structure queries it saw during training. It doesn't look at words in isolation; it looks at how 'config file' relates to 'Where is'."
